-- lib/kernel.cub — the smallest machine, written in the language (part of machine-in-hyperactive).
-- The paper's §4.5 for functions and sharing only. A wire is a slot of the memory: empty, holding a
-- value, holding an operation, or holding the two that met there (a ready pair). A value is a
-- closure (a body and the wires of its environment), a number, or a reference to a held wire; an
-- operation is apply, increment, duplicate, erase, or forward (a wire joined to a wire).
-- Three pieces, separate. nk-rule is every rule: given the two cells that met and the first wire
-- name it may use, it returns what to put on which wires, and reads nothing else. nk-plan is a step
-- at one wire: the pair there rewritten by its rule, or, for an operation facing a reference, the
-- held wire read (if nothing is held yet the plan is empty: waiting is not a step). nk-do is the
-- memory: each request changes one wire, by what was there. The executor (NkExec) is outside all
-- three: it chooses which wire steps next and hands out the wire names, so an executor for other
-- hardware is another choice over the same rules.
-- Not here: neutrals (a number applied, or a closure incremented, is dropped), dimensions, systems,
-- composition, data; held wires are never freed.
-- It loads after the library: the proofs use the library's lemmas on word equality (weq-sound), and
-- the C experiment that emits it is the library's.
import "library.cub"
